From b2ae1f6c1d079abb349000954cfa2c7212ba64f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NEMO Rung4 executor <executor@nemo.local>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 13:00:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] 3b: derive slow-coag K0 from the grid's grain number
 density (grid-aware)

The slow-limit target is t_run/t_coag, and t_coag = 1/(K0*n_grain) with
n_grain = (sum grain abundances/H)*n_gas, which depends on the bin count (at fixed
dust mass, more/smaller bins => more grains). The hardcoded K0 points (5e-18/5e-19),
tuned for the 3-5 bin fixture, fall below the solver noise floor (~rtol=1e-4) at the
20-bin production grid, so the linearity ratio read FAIL for a reason unrelated to
the physics (the reduction itself is fine: rA/rB=9.85 once K0 is above the floor).

Fix: measure n_grain from the reference run and set K0 = TARGET/(t_run*n_grain),
with TARGET (t_run/t_coag) chosen ~100x above the rtol noise floor and deep in the
linear regime; the second point is TARGET/10. This expresses "slow" in a
grid-independent way and keys the K0 points off the grid's own grain number rather
than two magic constants that only hold on one grid (the convergence study runs
non-fiducial grids). Rationale documented in the harness header.

Verified: PASS at both grids with auto-derived K0 -- native n_grain=200 cm^-3
-> K0_A=1.6e-17, rA/rB=9.94 ; 20-bin n_grain=14.4 cm^-3 -> K0_A=2.2e-16, rA/rB=9.32;
both at worst rel-diff ~1e-2, floor never triggered.
